          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense vector search to improve retrieval accuracy in RAG systems.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One substantive source describing hybrid retrieval pattern; reciprocal rank fusion technique well-established in IR literature.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n**Merging strategy:** Reciprocal rank fusion is the standard approach for combining the two ranked lists, balancing results from both methods without requiring score normalization.\\n\\n**Performance characteristics:** Hybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent and semantic meaning\\n\\nThis pattern has become the default retrieval approach in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Eval source: update-over-duplicate/sample-07 \\u2014 hybrid retrieval definition and performance characteristics\\n\u0022}"
